1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,800
 Hello and welcome back to the Articulate Journal.

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:09,360
 In the last episode, I explained why Articulate was being designed as an AI-native system

3
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:12,120
 and what I meant by AI-native architecture.

4
00:00:12,120 --> 00:00:18,480
 In this episode, I want to take a step back for a moment and look at something more fundamental.

5
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:22,240
 AI-native systems are still systems.

6
00:00:22,240 --> 00:00:25,480
 Every production system is ultimately judged by the same question.

7
00:00:25,480 --> 00:00:27,680
 Can people trust it?

8
00:00:27,680 --> 00:00:30,720
 That's where system characteristics become important.

9
00:00:30,720 --> 00:00:33,840
 There's a huge amount of excitement around AI at the moment.

10
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:38,440
 Every week there's a new model, a new framework or another agent platform promising to change

11
00:00:38,440 --> 00:00:40,800
 the way we build software.

12
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:46,200
 Those advances are genuinely exciting, but we must not forget the fundamentals of software

13
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:50,960
 engineering because once a system is in production, no one really cares how clever it is if they

14
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:52,800
 can't rely on it.

15
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:57,960
 They care whether it's available, whether it's secure, whether it performs well, whether

16
00:00:57,960 --> 00:01:03,840
 they can trust the answers it produces, whether someone can understand what happened when

17
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:05,200
 something goes wrong.

18
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:10,880
 In other words, they care about the characteristics of the system.

19
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:13,240
 Features make a system useful.

20
00:01:13,240 --> 00:01:16,160
 Characteristics make it trustworthy.

21
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:21,400
 Before we start exploring runtimes, workflows and multi-agent systems, I think it's worth

22
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:25,400
 taking a step back and asking a much simpler question.

23
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:29,440
 What qualities does every production system need to possess?

24
00:01:29,440 --> 00:01:33,320
 As architects, we tend to think about software in two dimensions.

25
00:01:33,320 --> 00:01:39,240
 The first is functionality, as in what does a system do, and the second is characteristics.

26
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:41,360
 How well does it do it?

27
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:46,080
 One thing that's always fascinated me about architecture is that we rarely choose patterns

28
00:01:46,080 --> 00:01:47,600
 for our own sake.

29
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:50,680
 We don't choose CQRS because it's fashionable.

30
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:53,800
 We don't choose event-sourcing because it's interesting.

31
00:01:53,800 --> 00:01:58,960
 We don't introduce durable workflows, simply because the technology exists.

32
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:03,200
 We choose architecture approaches because they improve characteristics that matter for

33
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:05,840
 the problem we're trying to solve.

34
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:09,680
 Every architectural decision is really a trade-off.

35
00:02:09,680 --> 00:02:13,920
 Improve one characteristic and another may become more difficult.

36
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:19,600
 Caching improves performance, but introduces consistency challenges.

37
00:02:19,600 --> 00:02:26,280
 Creating a system improves scalability while making observability and reliability harder.

38
00:02:26,280 --> 00:02:32,240
 Adding autonomous agents may increase flexibility, but reduce predictability.

39
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:35,600
 Architecture has never been about finding the perfect design.

40
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:39,000
 It's about deciding which trade-offs are appropriate.

41
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:42,160
 So let's look at some of those characteristics that matter.

42
00:02:42,160 --> 00:02:45,160
 The first is trust.

43
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:48,640
 Trust has always been fundamental to software.

44
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:55,080
 Trusters expect to protect their information, respect their permissions, record important

45
00:02:55,080 --> 00:02:57,920
 actions and behave safely.

46
00:02:57,920 --> 00:03:01,640
 AI native systems introduce new questions.

47
00:03:01,640 --> 00:03:04,840
 Which models are allowed to access this information?

48
00:03:04,840 --> 00:03:07,760
 Which tools can the agent invoke?

49
00:03:07,760 --> 00:03:11,480
 Can retrieved information cross-security boundaries?

50
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:14,920
 Can we explain why a particular answer was produced?

51
00:03:14,920 --> 00:03:19,720
 Can an agent perform an action without human approval?

52
00:03:19,720 --> 00:03:24,920
 Security is no longer about protecting APIs and databases, it's about protecting the intelligence

53
00:03:24,920 --> 00:03:28,280
 operating inside the system.

54
00:03:28,280 --> 00:03:33,520
 Being capable of doing something that is not the same as being authorized to do it.

55
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:35,400
 Next comes reliability.

56
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:39,480
 Eventually, every production system experiences failure.

57
00:03:39,480 --> 00:03:46,800
 A model provider becomes unavailable, a retrieval service times out, or a call to a tool fails,

58
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:49,760
 a workflow can be interrupted.

59
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:54,320
 Reliability isn't about pretending those failures won't happen, it's about deciding

60
00:03:54,320 --> 00:03:57,600
 what the system should do when they inevitably do.

61
00:03:57,600 --> 00:03:58,600
 Can we retry?

62
00:03:58,600 --> 00:04:01,680
 Can we continue using another model?

63
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:04,280
 Can it pause safely and resume later?

64
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:06,320
 Can it ask a human for help?

65
00:04:06,320 --> 00:04:10,320
 Or can it preserve enough state to continue without starting again?

66
00:04:10,320 --> 00:04:16,080
 That's what users experience as reliability.

67
00:04:16,080 --> 00:04:20,680
 Performance is another characteristic that becomes increasingly interesting in AI native

68
00:04:20,680 --> 00:04:21,680
 systems.

69
00:04:21,680 --> 00:04:24,800
 Traditionally, we measured things latency and throughput.

70
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:31,840
 Those still matter, but AI systems introduce a perceived performance.

71
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:35,960
 Streaming a response often feels much faster than waiting several seconds before anything

72
00:04:35,960 --> 00:04:36,960
 appears.

73
00:04:36,960 --> 00:04:42,520
 At the same time, reasoning itself has become part of the execution path.

74
00:04:42,520 --> 00:04:47,960
 Retrieval, cooling tools, validation, and model inference all contribute to the overall

75
00:04:47,960 --> 00:04:50,000
 experience.

76
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:55,400
 Optimizing one stage doesn't necessarily make the system feel faster, and performance always

77
00:04:55,400 --> 00:04:59,200
 needs to be considered alongside cost.

78
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:03,040
 Sometimes a larger model is worth using, sometimes a smaller model is more than good

79
00:05:03,040 --> 00:05:05,640
 enough.

80
00:05:05,640 --> 00:05:07,720
 Data security has also changed.

81
00:05:07,720 --> 00:05:11,160
 Cloud native systems taught us how to scale infrastructure.

82
00:05:11,160 --> 00:05:14,480
 AI native systems introduce another question.

83
00:05:14,480 --> 00:05:16,960
 How do we scale intelligence?

84
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:21,080
 One option is to build a single increasingly capable agent.

85
00:05:21,080 --> 00:05:26,840
 Give it more tools, more memory, a larger context, a more capable model.

86
00:05:26,840 --> 00:05:32,520
 At first, this seems attractive because everything happens in one place, but eventually the context

87
00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:37,800
 grows, the prompt becomes more complicated, tool selection becomes harder, and the agent

88
00:05:37,800 --> 00:05:42,760
 spends more effort managing its own knowledge and solving the problem.

89
00:05:42,760 --> 00:05:45,040
 The alternative is to scale out.

90
00:05:45,040 --> 00:05:49,600
 Instead of one larger agent, we create several specialized ones.

91
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:54,600
 Each has a focus responsibility, a smaller context, and a clearer purpose.

92
00:05:54,600 --> 00:06:00,560
 That often improves clarity and isolation, but it introduces another kind of complexity.

93
00:06:00,560 --> 00:06:05,320
 Now the architecture has to decide which agent should receive the request.

94
00:06:05,320 --> 00:06:11,720
 How context is transferred, how work is coordinated, how failures are recovered, and how reasoning

95
00:06:11,720 --> 00:06:13,940
 is observed across multiple agents.

96
00:06:13,940 --> 00:06:19,840
 In other words, scale out moves complexity from inside the agent into the architecture

97
00:06:19,840 --> 00:06:21,400
 itself.

98
00:06:21,400 --> 00:06:23,880
 Neither approach is automatically right.

99
00:06:23,880 --> 00:06:28,280
 Just as with microservices, the goal isn't to create the biggest component or the greatest

100
00:06:28,280 --> 00:06:30,080
 number of components.

101
00:06:30,080 --> 00:06:36,540
 The goal is to create or to place complexity where it can be understood and managed.

102
00:06:36,540 --> 00:06:42,840
 That leads to a principle I've started using throughout Articulate, coherence before size.

103
00:06:42,840 --> 00:06:45,300
 An agent should remain coherent.

104
00:06:45,300 --> 00:06:50,240
 Its responsibilities, tools, and contexts should naturally belong together.

105
00:06:50,240 --> 00:06:54,960
 Splitting an agent simply because smaller agents appear more sophisticated usually creates

106
00:06:54,960 --> 00:06:57,360
 more problems than it solves.

107
00:06:57,360 --> 00:07:03,040
 Selfability becomes even more important once intelligence becomes part of the runtime.

108
00:07:03,040 --> 00:07:05,880
 Traditional systems gives us logs, metrics, and traces.

109
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:12,360
 AI-native systems also need to understand prompts, retrieve knowledge, routing decisions, tools

110
00:07:12,360 --> 00:07:18,980
 that have been called, reasoning paths, confidence, and evaluation results.

111
00:07:18,980 --> 00:07:23,440
 When an answer is wrong, knowing that one service called another isn't enough.

112
00:07:23,440 --> 00:07:27,640
 You need to understand why that decision was made.

113
00:07:27,640 --> 00:07:33,440
 Another characteristic that becomes increasingly important is a volvability.

114
00:07:33,440 --> 00:07:35,000
 Models will change.

115
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:37,080
 Prompt strategies will change.

116
00:07:37,080 --> 00:07:38,720
 Frameworks will change.

117
00:07:38,720 --> 00:07:43,840
 The architecture shouldn't depend on today's implementation choices.

118
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:48,120
 Responsibilities should remain understandable even if tomorrow's runtime looks completely

119
00:07:48,120 --> 00:07:50,720
 different.

120
00:07:50,720 --> 00:07:53,120
 Empathy matters for the same reason.

121
00:07:53,120 --> 00:07:55,680
 Someone has to support these systems.

122
00:07:55,680 --> 00:08:00,680
 Operators may need to disable a model, change routing policies, inspect a reasoning path,

123
00:08:00,680 --> 00:08:03,280
 or replay a failed execution.

124
00:08:03,280 --> 00:08:09,040
 An elegant architecture that can't be operated effectively rarely succeeds.

125
00:08:09,040 --> 00:08:14,440
 Finally, there's one characteristic that I think AI-native systems have brought much

126
00:08:14,440 --> 00:08:18,760
 more clearly into focus, evaluation.

127
00:08:18,760 --> 00:08:25,760
 Learning software engineering gives us testing, unit tests, integration, and performance tests,

128
00:08:25,760 --> 00:08:26,760
 etc.

129
00:08:26,760 --> 00:08:33,360
 Those remain essential, but AI systems introduce probabilistic behavior.

130
00:08:33,360 --> 00:08:38,200
 A workflow can complete successfully and still produce a poor answer.

131
00:08:38,200 --> 00:08:41,600
 The software worked, the behavior didn't.

132
00:08:41,600 --> 00:08:47,440
 Testing tells us whether the implementation functions correctly.

133
00:08:47,440 --> 00:08:52,920
 Evaluation tells us whether the system actually achieved its intended outcome.

134
00:08:52,920 --> 00:08:58,640
 I think evaluation will become one of the defining characteristics for AI-native systems,

135
00:08:58,640 --> 00:09:03,640
 not because testing becomes less important, but because trustworthy behavior becomes just

136
00:09:03,640 --> 00:09:06,680
 as important as correct execution.

137
00:09:06,680 --> 00:09:11,040
 Ultimately, that's where the next episode is really about.

138
00:09:11,040 --> 00:09:16,760
 AI-native systems introduce new technologies, new runtimes, and new patterns.

139
00:09:16,760 --> 00:09:23,360
 But they don't replace the foundations of software architecture they build upon them.

140
00:09:23,360 --> 00:09:26,440
 Remember AI-native systems are still systems.

141
00:09:26,440 --> 00:09:32,480
 They're judged by their reliability, their security, their scalability, their observability,

142
00:09:32,480 --> 00:09:35,400
 and above all, whether people can trust them.

143
00:09:35,400 --> 00:09:40,480
 Those characteristics don't become less important because intelligence has entered the architecture.

144
00:09:40,480 --> 00:09:42,760
 They become even more important.

145
00:09:42,760 --> 00:09:48,080
 In the next episode, we'll build on these ideas by looking at the architectural principles

146
00:09:48,080 --> 00:09:52,680
 that are beginning to shape, articulate, and why thinking in terms of capabilities rather

147
00:09:52,680 --> 00:09:57,280
 than components provides a stronger foundation for building AI-native systems.

